= Priming — Simple =

'''Priming''' happens when seeing or processing something changes how quickly or easily you process something later.

If you recently saw a word, you may recognize it faster the next time.

Seeing one word can also make a related word easier to process. For example, “doctor” can make “nurse” easier to recognize in some tasks.

Priming is not the same as persuasion or mind control.

Some types of priming are very well established in laboratory tasks. Broader claims that tiny hidden cues strongly control complicated social behavior are much less certain.

